CAT = "cparegProcedure"; SEG = "PRO"
Q = [
("After receiving a statutory notice of deficiency, a taxpayer in the United States who wants Tax Court review without paying first must petition within:",
 "90 days", ["30 days", "60 days", "180 days"],
 "The notice of deficiency, often called the 90-day letter, gives 90 days (150 if addressed outside the United States) to petition the Tax Court. The 30-day letter comes earlier and offers Appeals."),
("An IRS 30-day letter after an audit gives the taxpayer the chance to:",
 "Request an Appeals conference", ["Petition the U.S. Tax Court", "File suit in district court", "Settle with no interest due"],
 "The 30-day letter proposes the adjustments and lets the taxpayer protest to the IRS Independent Office of Appeals. Tax Court review follows the later 90-day letter."),
("A taxpayer who wants a jury to decide a federal tax dispute must:",
 "Pay, then sue in district court", ["Petition the U.S. Tax Court", "Sue in the Court of Federal Claims", "Elect the small case procedure"],
 "Only a U.S. district court offers a jury trial in a tax refund suit, and the tax must be paid first. The Tax Court and the Court of Federal Claims sit without juries."),
("A decision of the U.S. Tax Court in a regular case is appealed to the:",
 "Regional court of appeals", ["U.S. Supreme Court directly", "U.S. district court", "U.S. Court of Federal Claims"],
 "Regular Tax Court decisions go to the U.S. court of appeals for the circuit where the taxpayer resides. Small case decisions cannot be appealed."),
("Absent fraud, a failure to file or a large omission, the IRS must assess additional income tax within how long after the return is filed?",
 "3 years", ["2 years", "6 years", "7 years"],
 "The general assessment period is 3 years from the later of the filing date or the due date. Fraud or no return leaves it open indefinitely."),
("A taxpayer omits gross income of more than 25% of the gross income reported on the return. The IRS may assess within:",
 "6 years", ["3 years", "10 years", "7 years"],
 "A substantial omission of more than 25% of reported gross income extends the assessment period to 6 years."),
("Ana files her calendar-year individual return on February 20. Her 3-year assessment period starts on:",
 "The April 15 due date", ["February 20, when filed", "January 1 of that year", "December 31 of that year"],
 "A return filed before its due date is treated as filed on the due date, so the period runs from April 15."),
("A taxpayer filed on time and paid in full with the return. A claim for refund must be filed within the later of 3 years from filing or:",
 "2 years from payment", ["1 year from payment", "3 years from payment", "6 months from payment"],
 "A refund claim is timely if filed within 3 years of the return or 2 years of payment, whichever is later."),
("A taxpayer files 3 months late with no extension and no reasonable cause and owes tax. Before any offset, the failure-to-file penalty is:",
 "15% of the unpaid tax", ["5% of the unpaid tax", "1.5% of the unpaid tax", "25% of the unpaid tax"],
 "The failure-to-file penalty is 5% of the unpaid tax per month or part of a month, up to 25%. Three months gives 15%, reduced by any failure-to-pay penalty for the same months."),
("Travel and gift expenses covered by section 274(d) must be supported by records showing the amount, the time and place, and the:",
 "Business purpose", ["Payment method", "Name of a witness", "Supplier's tax ID"],
 "Section 274(d) requires adequate records of amount, time, place and business purpose (and business relationship for gifts). Estimates under the Cohan rule are not allowed for these expenses."),
("The accuracy-related penalty for negligence or a substantial understatement of income tax is generally:",
 "20% of the underpayment", ["75% of the underpayment", "40% of the underpayment", "10% of the underpayment"],
 "Section 6662 imposes 20% on the part of an underpayment due to negligence or a substantial understatement. 40% applies to gross valuation misstatements."),
("If part of an underpayment is due to fraud, the civil fraud penalty on that part is:",
 "75%", ["20%", "40%", "100%"],
 "Section 6663 imposes a 75% penalty on the portion of the underpayment attributable to fraud. The IRS must prove fraud by clear and convincing evidence."),
("Which source carries the most weight in the hierarchy of federal tax authority?",
 "The Internal Revenue Code", ["A final Treasury regulation", "A revenue ruling", "A private letter ruling"],
 "The Code is the statute and sits at the top. Treasury regulations interpret it, followed by revenue rulings and procedures; letter rulings bind only the requester."),
("A private letter ruling may be relied on as precedent by:",
 "Only the taxpayer who asked", ["Any taxpayer with like facts", "Taxpayers in the same circuit", "Any taxpayer once released"],
 "A private letter ruling applies only to the taxpayer who requested it. Others may read it for insight but cannot cite it as precedent."),
("A taxpayer takes a return position with a reasonable basis but not substantial authority. To avoid the substantial understatement penalty, the taxpayer can:",
 "Disclose it on Form 8275", ["Attach the source invoices", "File the return early", "Have a CPA sign the return"],
 "Adequate disclosure on Form 8275 (8275-R for positions contrary to a regulation) avoids the penalty for a position with a reasonable basis."),
("A U.S. person must file an FBAR when the total value of foreign financial accounts at any time during the year exceeds:",
 "$10,000", ["$50,000", "$5,000", "$100,000"],
 "FinCEN Form 114 is required when the aggregate maximum value of foreign financial accounts exceeds $10,000 at any time in the calendar year."),
]
